<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hellenistic period</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hellenistic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hellenistic period is the era from the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 323 BCE to the late first century BCE, when Greek culture spread across the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and western Asia. It was dominated by the successor kingdoms, including the Ptolemaic and Seleucid empires. Hellenistic influence also reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period.jpg" alt="Hellenistic period" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nike_of_Samothrake_Louvre_Ma2369_n4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Hellenistic period</b> Tanagra gave its name to the painted terracotta figurines found in its graves.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Hellenistic period</b> Byzantium controlled the Hieron, a sanctuary of Zeus Ourios at the Black Sea entrance of the Bosporus where ships waited for favourable winds.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>These accounts were written many centuries later and mix history with legend, but archaeology confirms that a centralised kingdom with a fortified royal seat at Mtskheta and the acropolis of Armaztsikhe was taking shape in the early <b>Hellenistic period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A revolt led by the Lydian Pactyes was soon crushed, and Lydian independence was never restored, although the language and culture survived into the <b>Hellenistic period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Hellenistic period</b> Cretan mercenaries, especially archers, served across the eastern Mediterranean, and piracy and slave raiding became a significant, and notorious, part of the island's economy, in which Knossos shared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ancient writers describe the constitution as a moderate democracy dominated in practice by the wealthy, and it proved unusually stable: Rhodes avoided the tyrannies and violent revolutions common in Greek cities during the <b>Hellenistic period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
